WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.603
A prompt is a spec, and the reader is new here.

2
00:00:02.883 --> 00:00:12.056
This week treats prompts the way you treat requirements documents: as specifications that someone else has to execute without asking you questions.

3
00:00:12.336 --> 00:00:16.453
Anthropic’s guidance offers a framing that fits a PM’s instincts.

4
00:00:16.733 --> 00:00:26.355
Picture the model as a brilliant new employee: highly capable, but on day one, with no knowledge of your customers, your norms, or what you did last quarter.

5
00:00:26.635 --> 00:00:28.768
Everything it needs has to be on the page.

6
00:00:29.048 --> 00:00:31.373
That framing comes with a practical test.

7
00:00:31.653 --> 00:00:37.285
Before you send a prompt, imagine handing it to a capable colleague who has never seen the project.

8
00:00:37.565 --> 00:00:42.323
Would they know what to produce, for whom, in what format, and how to tell when they are done?

9
00:00:42.603 --> 00:00:50.453
If they would come back with questions, the model will guess at the answers instead, and guesses are where inconsistent output comes from.

10
00:00:50.733 --> 00:01:04.557
So a good prompt usually states four things: the task (what to produce), the context (who it is for and what situation it sits in), the constraints (length, scope, rules), and the format (exactly what the output looks like).

11
00:01:04.837 --> 00:01:06.587
Specificity beats brevity.

12
00:01:06.867 --> 00:01:21.416
“Summarize this status update” leaves a dozen decisions to chance; “Summarize this status update for the VP of Operations in three bullets: what shipped, what slipped and why, and the one decision you need from her” leaves almost none.

13
00:01:21.696 --> 00:01:27.456
One more principle from Anthropic’s docs: if you want more than the minimum, ask for it explicitly.

14
00:01:27.736 --> 00:01:37.336
Current models tend to follow instructions closely and literally, so “go beyond the basics” or “include edge cases” has to be said, not implied.
